WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.460
Imagine waking up, you know, going through your morning routine with just absolute precision.

00:04.660 --> 00:06.080
Right. The usual daily grind.

00:06.220 --> 00:10.560
Yeah, exactly. You commute to your job. You execute your tasks flawlessly.

00:11.040 --> 00:15.380
You hit every single metric. Your boss praises your reliability, right?

00:15.700 --> 00:16.760
The dream employee.

00:17.020 --> 00:20.760
Totally. And then you go home. You play a little video game to unwind.

00:20.840 --> 00:23.980
You go to sleep and you wake up and do it all again.

00:24.320 --> 00:25.860
Sounds pretty normal, honestly.

00:25.860 --> 00:37.180
It does. But now imagine discovering that, structurally speaking, your entire cognitive existence is completely identical to a background character in a video game, like a literal NPC.

00:37.520 --> 00:39.440
Yeah, that is a genuinely unsettling premise.

00:39.700 --> 00:53.300
It really is. Today we're exploring this staggering piece of research that argues the way we design our work, our education, and even like our hobbies might actually be actively stripping away our capacity for independent thought.

00:53.300 --> 00:58.340
Right. And replacing it with this highly rewarded, heavily supervised monotony.

00:58.880 --> 01:11.140
Which is wild. So welcome to today's Deep Dive. We are incredibly excited to have you with us because the stack of material on the table today is the kind of thing that makes you look at your own life through a completely different lens.

01:11.420 --> 01:19.320
It really does. We usually think of becoming a mindless automaton as something that happens when we fail, you know, or when we just check out entirely.

01:19.320 --> 01:32.280
Right, like we gave up. Exactly. But the research we're looking at suggests that this erasure of the self actually happens most effectively when we are successfully performing the exact tasks society asks of us.

01:32.680 --> 01:46.360
So we're unpacking a 2026 paper by the independent researcher Fleishen. It's titled, let me read this correctly, Magnificent Monotony and the Non-Player Character, Swatting, Grinding, and the Supervision of Purpose.

01:46.360 --> 01:49.140
A very dense title, but a brilliant paper.

01:49.340 --> 02:03.760
Super dense. But our mission today is to dig into this wild structural similarity between a 19th century scholar agonizing over Greek grammar and a 21st century gamer spending hours trying to level up their digital character.

02:03.760 --> 02:17.320
And through that comparison, we want to help you figure out if your daily routines are secretly building you into a powerful independent thinker or, well, just quietly turning you into an interchangeable part in someone else's machine.

02:17.580 --> 02:25.720
Okay, let's unpack this. Because the jump from 19th century academic discipline to modern gaming culture is a huge leap.

02:25.880 --> 02:27.020
It's massive, yeah.

02:27.020 --> 02:30.100
Where does this paper even begin to bridge that gap?

02:30.420 --> 02:33.760
Well, it actually begins in the posthumous notes of Friedrich Nietzsche.

02:34.040 --> 02:35.700
Oh, wow. Okay. Starting heavy.

02:35.960 --> 02:45.800
Yeah. Specifically, a collection gathered under the title, The Will to Power. We're looking really closely at the Anthony Ludovici translation, focusing on aphorism 888.

02:46.220 --> 02:46.640
Got it.

02:46.640 --> 02:58.680
So, in this note, Nietzsche's reflecting on the classical philologist. And philology, which is the study of language in historical sources, was actually Nietzsche's own profession before he shifted entirely into philosophy.

02:58.920 --> 02:59.540
Right, right.

02:59.920 --> 03:04.720
And in this note, he refers to the philologist as the educator per se.

03:05.560 --> 03:09.540
Which, on the surface, I mean, that sounds like he's just patting his own profession on the back.

03:09.540 --> 03:14.240
Like, the philologist is the ultimate teacher, the absolute peak of the academic mountain.

03:14.720 --> 03:20.200
It absolutely sounds like praise. That is, until you look at the actual mechanism of that education.

03:21.180 --> 03:23.520
Nietzsche's observation here is intensely cynical.

03:23.800 --> 03:26.260
Because it's Nietzsche. So, of course it is.

03:26.680 --> 03:33.180
Grackley. He argues that the philologist is the ultimate educator not because of the brilliant truths they impart,

03:33.180 --> 03:40.820
but because their specific field provides the absolute best template for what he calls magnificent monotony.

03:41.740 --> 03:44.060
Magnificent monotony. What a phrase.

03:44.340 --> 03:44.880
It really is.

03:45.440 --> 03:55.820
Under the strict guidance of the philologist, the youth are taught to, and the Ludovici translation uses a wonderful archaic term here, in quotation marks, to swat.

03:56.160 --> 03:58.620
Wait, swat? Like hitting a fly?

03:58.620 --> 04:06.280
No, no. It's spelled S-W-A-T, which is actually an older spelling of the British term swat, S-W-O-T.

04:06.420 --> 04:09.420
Oh, okay. I've heard that used in, like, British media.

04:09.740 --> 04:12.760
A swat is essentially someone who studies obsessively, right?

04:13.200 --> 04:18.180
It implies this kind of laborious, brute force drilling of information.

04:18.560 --> 04:19.440
Exactly that.

04:19.920 --> 04:23.640
Swatting is this grueling, heavily repetitive study of a subject that

04:23.640 --> 04:28.040
doesn't inherently hold any immediate burning interest for the student.

04:28.040 --> 04:30.720
Right. Just rote memorization.

04:31.020 --> 04:33.220
Yeah. It is discipline for discipline's sake.

04:33.740 --> 04:37.700
And Nietzsche points out that this specific flavor of magnificent monotony

04:37.700 --> 04:43.420
is the very first prerequisite for anyone who will later need to fulfill mechanical duties in society.

04:43.660 --> 04:46.720
Right. Because society needs people to do the boring stuff.

04:46.940 --> 04:47.340
Exactly.

04:47.600 --> 04:50.460
He actually lists the roles this training prepares you for.

04:50.460 --> 04:57.500
He says, state officials, husbands, slaves of the desk, newspaper readers, and soldiers.

04:58.000 --> 05:04.280
That is a wild list. He's lumping in husbands and newspaper readers with soldiers.

05:04.700 --> 05:06.120
Yeah. He's not pulling any punches.

05:06.120 --> 05:10.480
I mean, what he is saying is that this isn't an education to make you a genius.

05:10.720 --> 05:14.960
It's a training regimen designed to build your capacity for sheer endurance.

05:15.480 --> 05:16.680
That is the core insight.

05:17.400 --> 05:20.800
And this is where the paper makes its first massive conceptual leap.

05:21.440 --> 05:27.120
The author argues that this 19th century concept of swatting through magnificent monotony

05:27.120 --> 05:30.640
has a direct one-to-one equivalent in our modern era.

05:31.000 --> 05:32.540
Okay. I think I see where this is going.

05:32.540 --> 05:37.980
Yep. If you step into the world of online role-playing games, or honestly, even just mobile games,

05:38.500 --> 05:41.740
this exact behavioral structure is known as grinding.

05:42.180 --> 05:46.160
Oh, man. If you have ever played a video game, you know the grind in your bones.

05:46.400 --> 05:47.000
You really do.

05:47.680 --> 05:51.000
Structurally, a grind is a highly repetitive loop.

05:51.240 --> 05:55.760
You are executing a specific, usually tedious action, like, I don't know,

05:55.840 --> 06:00.860
fighting the same digital goblin over and over, or clicking a rock to mine digital ore.

06:00.960 --> 06:02.100
Right. Doing it hundreds of times.

06:02.100 --> 06:06.900
Yeah, and you are doing it to earn these tiny, incremental amounts of a measured quantity.

06:07.360 --> 06:09.640
Experience points, digital gold, whatever it is.

06:10.100 --> 06:15.720
You literally just watch a bar slowly fill up so you can unlock a reward that is entirely

06:15.720 --> 06:17.780
deferred to a later time.

06:17.960 --> 06:23.020
And what is genuinely fascinating here is how the source material strips away the cultural aesthetics

06:23.020 --> 06:25.480
to show us the raw mechanics underneath.

06:25.680 --> 06:27.180
Right, because the aesthetics are so different.

06:27.180 --> 06:33.080
Exactly. It doesn't matter if we are looking at a Victorian student being forced to conjugate

06:33.080 --> 06:38.560
irregular Greek verbs by candlelight, or a modern teenager sitting in front of a glowing screen

06:38.560 --> 06:41.620
fighting identical digital monsters for six hours.

06:41.880 --> 06:43.920
Structurally, it's the exact same thing.

06:44.080 --> 06:44.340
Yes.

06:44.340 --> 06:49.360
The paper identifies three shared structural elements that define both activities.

06:50.000 --> 06:54.760
First, the individual instances of the activity are intrinsically uninteresting.

06:55.020 --> 06:59.480
Like, conjugating the 50th verb is just as dull as slaying the 50th goblin.

06:59.760 --> 07:01.500
Right. The action itself holds no joy.

07:02.540 --> 07:06.120
Second, the value of the activity is entirely deferred.

07:06.340 --> 07:08.340
You're not getting anything out of it in the moment.

07:08.720 --> 07:10.900
You're paying a cost now for a payoff later.

07:10.900 --> 07:13.020
Okay, that makes sense. And the third?

07:13.620 --> 07:16.280
The third, and this is the most critical piece for our discussion today,

07:17.080 --> 07:21.700
the primary effect on the person doing it is to produce a disposition to continue acting,

07:22.200 --> 07:25.340
even in the complete absence of personal interest.

07:25.700 --> 07:30.820
Wow. So it's really about the form of the container, not the liquid inside it.

07:31.440 --> 07:31.980
Precisely.

07:32.280 --> 07:37.740
The activity provides a pattern of applying effort when there is zero internal motivation to keep you going.

07:38.300 --> 07:39.980
That is precisely the author's point.

07:39.980 --> 07:44.800
But, you know, I know you are a big reader of game design theory,

07:44.960 --> 07:47.900
so I'm really curious how this sits with you.

07:48.040 --> 07:48.440
Oh, yeah.

07:48.720 --> 07:53.300
Because there is a very famous definition of a game by the philosopher Bernard Suits

07:53.300 --> 07:58.500
that seems to really contradict this idea of the grind as just miserable training.

07:59.080 --> 08:00.380
I am so glad you brought up Suits,

08:00.480 --> 08:03.760
because I was genuinely stuck on this when I was reading the paper.

08:04.040 --> 08:05.860
It's a glaring contradiction to first glance.

08:05.940 --> 08:06.840
It really is.

08:06.840 --> 08:10.200
So Suits famously defined playing a game as a, quote,

08:10.300 --> 08:13.840
voluntary attempt to overcome unnecessary obstacles.

08:14.680 --> 08:14.780
Right.

08:14.900 --> 08:16.900
So, like, if I am playing golf,

08:17.080 --> 08:20.960
the absolute most efficient way to get the ball in the hole is to just pick it up,

08:21.420 --> 08:22.680
walk over, and drop it in.

08:22.780 --> 08:23.580
But that wouldn't be golf.

08:23.760 --> 08:24.140
Exactly.

08:24.140 --> 08:30.140
I voluntarily accept the totally unnecessary obstacle of hitting it with a metal stick

08:30.140 --> 08:31.480
from hundreds of yards away.

08:32.400 --> 08:37.420
So, if I choose to sit down and grind out experience points in a role-playing game,

08:37.820 --> 08:40.600
isn't that just me voluntarily accepting an obstacle?

08:40.960 --> 08:41.840
It's a fair question.

08:42.180 --> 08:42.480
Right.

08:42.600 --> 08:45.340
Like, why is the paper treating the grind like it's some oppressive,

08:45.500 --> 08:48.800
soul-crushing mechanism if I am actively opting into it?

08:48.800 --> 08:50.680
It is a really sharp pushback,

08:50.840 --> 08:54.680
and the paper actually addresses the Suits definition directly to resolve this tension.

08:55.320 --> 09:01.220
The author argues that a grind does retain the form of voluntariness that Suits talks about.

09:01.300 --> 09:02.540
Okay, so I am choosing it.

09:02.760 --> 09:03.040
Yes.

09:03.280 --> 09:04.080
You boot up the game,

09:04.380 --> 09:06.160
you choose to swing the digital pickaxe,

09:06.300 --> 09:09.320
and you can, in theory, close the software whenever you want.

09:10.200 --> 09:15.280
But the grind hollows out the core interest in overcoming the obstacle.

09:15.580 --> 09:16.280
Oh, I see.

09:16.280 --> 09:20.300
When you are truly playing a game, wrestling with the obstacle itself is engaging.

09:20.860 --> 09:23.180
In a grind, the obstacle isn't engaging at all.

09:23.620 --> 09:25.600
It is just a tollbooth.

09:25.860 --> 09:27.420
Oh, that makes so much sense.

09:28.040 --> 09:29.160
A tollbooth isn't a challenge.

09:29.440 --> 09:31.400
It's literally just a tax on your time.

09:31.740 --> 09:32.860
That's a great way to put it.

09:33.220 --> 09:35.620
And furthermore, and this is the crucial distinction,

09:36.340 --> 09:39.380
the meter you were filling up and the reward you were striving for

09:39.380 --> 09:41.300
belong entirely to the designer.

09:41.540 --> 09:43.640
Right, you didn't invent the experience bar.

09:44.060 --> 09:44.500
Exactly.

09:44.500 --> 09:47.040
You are exercising your freedom, sure,

09:47.380 --> 09:51.180
but you are doing it within a tightly confined artificial space

09:51.180 --> 09:54.880
where the ends were completely laid down by someone else.

09:55.400 --> 09:57.660
You're paying the psychological price of repetition

09:57.660 --> 10:02.080
to purchase a good that someone else priced and defined.

10:02.460 --> 10:04.040
That reframes it completely for me.

10:04.040 --> 10:08.980
Like, if I voluntarily practice a guitar chord progression for six months

10:08.980 --> 10:11.040
because I want to write a song for my wife,

10:11.560 --> 10:13.280
the repetition might be super tedious,

10:13.580 --> 10:16.480
but the meter and the reward belong to me.

10:16.600 --> 10:17.480
Yes, exactly.

10:17.820 --> 10:21.060
The grind is when I surrender the ownership of the reward to a system.

10:21.340 --> 10:23.500
The designer essentially sets the curriculum.

10:23.500 --> 10:26.860
Exactly, which leads us to a much deeper question.

10:27.180 --> 10:31.480
If this magnificent monotony is inherently uninteresting

10:31.480 --> 10:34.680
and it fundamentally drains our intrinsic motivation,

10:35.180 --> 10:36.520
why do we do it?

10:36.720 --> 10:38.000
Yeah, why are we obsessed with it?

10:38.120 --> 10:38.260
Right.

10:38.940 --> 10:42.320
Why is human society, from the universities of 19th century Europe

10:42.320 --> 10:45.140
to the tech campuses of Silicon Valley today,

10:45.720 --> 10:48.540
leaned so heavily into this specific structure?

10:48.540 --> 10:50.440
And to get to the bottom of that,

10:50.560 --> 10:54.680
the paper takes this really interesting biographical detour

10:54.680 --> 10:56.260
into Nietzsche's life.

10:56.660 --> 10:58.460
It does, and it provides vital context.

10:59.100 --> 11:00.680
As we mentioned, Nietzsche spent a decade

11:00.680 --> 11:04.240
as a professor of classical philology at the University of Basel.

11:04.280 --> 11:05.700
It was just pretty prestigious, right?

11:05.980 --> 11:06.460
Extremely.

11:06.740 --> 11:08.180
He was actually a prodigy,

11:08.680 --> 11:11.180
appointed to the chair at an incredibly young age.

11:11.620 --> 11:14.180
So when he writes about the educator per se,

11:14.860 --> 11:17.080
he isn't lobbing criticisms from the cheap seats.

11:17.080 --> 11:18.520
He's not just an angry outsider.

11:18.980 --> 11:19.320
Not at all.

11:19.700 --> 11:20.960
He is an ultimate insider

11:20.960 --> 11:23.000
who has become profoundly skeptical

11:23.000 --> 11:25.240
of the very machine he operates.

11:25.960 --> 11:29.200
He is looking at this massive, socially revered institution,

11:29.600 --> 11:33.240
and he realizes its actual functional output

11:33.240 --> 11:35.720
isn't deep understanding or enlightenment.

11:36.040 --> 11:36.320
Right.

11:36.720 --> 11:38.680
Its output is endurance.

11:39.240 --> 11:41.280
He is seeing the inner workings of the factory,

11:41.280 --> 11:43.520
and he realizes they aren't manufacturing scholars,

11:43.720 --> 11:45.460
they are manufacturing compliance.

11:45.460 --> 11:47.980
That is a brilliant way to put it.

11:48.540 --> 11:50.640
And the paper does something analytically brilliant here

11:50.640 --> 11:53.780
to explain how this factory works on different people.

11:53.920 --> 11:55.800
Because it doesn't affect everyone the same way.

11:55.860 --> 11:56.200
Exactly.

11:56.320 --> 11:56.460
Yeah.

11:56.840 --> 11:59.800
It breaks down the function of identical mechanical activities

11:59.800 --> 12:02.260
by looking at who is performing them.

12:02.380 --> 12:05.560
It categorizes the performers into three distinct groups.

12:06.120 --> 12:06.340
Okay.

12:06.480 --> 12:07.140
Let's go through them.

12:07.140 --> 12:10.060
First of all, the first group is the young, right?

12:10.060 --> 12:14.480
For them, magnificent monotony serves the function of endurance.

12:15.000 --> 12:17.220
This goes back to that list of future occupations, right?

12:17.280 --> 12:18.920
The clerks, soldiers, husbands.

12:19.340 --> 12:20.260
Yes, precisely.

12:20.920 --> 12:24.280
For the young, it's a training regimen designed to build the disposition

12:24.280 --> 12:26.020
to endure tedious tasks.

12:26.020 --> 12:29.640
Society requires a massive base layer of people

12:29.640 --> 12:32.520
who will not just quit because they are bored.

12:33.240 --> 12:33.260
Right.

12:33.340 --> 12:36.120
The world would collapse if everyone quit when things got boring.

12:36.320 --> 12:36.720
Exactly.

12:37.520 --> 12:39.800
The routine is literally altering their disposition,

12:40.240 --> 12:42.260
molding them to tolerate friction

12:42.260 --> 12:45.200
so the bureaucratic and military structures of society

12:45.200 --> 12:46.440
can actually function.

12:46.980 --> 12:47.320
Okay.

12:47.420 --> 12:48.980
So that's the first group, the young.

12:49.280 --> 12:51.260
What happens when that training is complete

12:51.260 --> 12:53.500
and you are just an adult out in the world?

12:53.500 --> 12:56.460
That brings us to the second group, the exhausted.

12:57.360 --> 12:57.760
Oh boy.

12:57.900 --> 12:59.040
I feel this one coming.

12:59.260 --> 13:00.300
Yeah, it hits close to home.

13:01.080 --> 13:03.280
Here the text pulls in another of Nietzsche's aphorisms,

13:03.660 --> 13:04.440
aphorism 45.

13:05.220 --> 13:09.000
He discusses something he calls the hygiene of the week.

13:09.140 --> 13:10.380
The hygiene of the week.

13:10.700 --> 13:12.120
That sounds so pejorative.

13:12.540 --> 13:13.000
It does.

13:13.420 --> 13:15.820
But he's applying this to someone who is overwhelmed,

13:16.960 --> 13:20.800
someone who reacts too readily to every external stimulus

13:20.800 --> 13:24.680
and is absolutely depleted by the constant daily demand

13:24.680 --> 13:25.480
to make decisions.

13:25.820 --> 13:26.760
Decision fatigue.

13:27.280 --> 13:27.700
Exactly.

13:28.400 --> 13:32.100
For this exhausted person, mechanical activity isn't training,

13:32.360 --> 13:33.120
it is a remedy.

13:33.960 --> 13:34.860
It provides relief.

13:35.440 --> 13:38.360
This feels aggressively relevant today.

13:38.940 --> 13:41.660
I mean, it completely removes the demand to choose.

13:41.960 --> 13:42.560
Yes, it does.

13:42.800 --> 13:44.380
If you are listening to this right now,

13:44.420 --> 13:46.280
just think about decision fatigue.

13:46.280 --> 13:48.680
You finish a brutal 10-hour shift at work

13:48.680 --> 13:51.480
where you had to make like 100 micro decisions.

13:51.840 --> 13:55.940
You get home and the idea of watching a dense prestige television show

13:55.940 --> 13:58.740
with a complicated plot or reading a challenging novel

13:58.740 --> 14:00.640
is physically repulsive.

14:01.160 --> 14:02.700
You just don't have the bandwidth.

14:03.480 --> 14:03.880
Exactly.

14:04.200 --> 14:04.800
So what do you do?

14:04.900 --> 14:07.820
You open a simple color matching game on your phone

14:07.820 --> 14:10.320
or you mindlessly sort your email inbox

14:10.320 --> 14:12.080
or you just doom scroll.

14:12.560 --> 14:12.780
Right.

14:12.860 --> 14:16.100
You seek out an activity where the rules are entirely fixed in advance.

14:16.100 --> 14:18.760
And each step automatically dictates the next.

14:19.740 --> 14:22.520
That is the perfect modern translation of Nietzsche's point.

14:23.460 --> 14:27.040
For the exhausted, the routine postpones the terrifying exercise of choice.

14:27.640 --> 14:30.380
It is therapeutic because it suppresses the demand

14:30.380 --> 14:34.080
to reconsider your actions for as long as the routine lasts.

14:34.840 --> 14:35.320
Wow.

14:35.640 --> 14:39.080
It is essentially a vacation from autonomy.

14:39.440 --> 14:41.600
A vacation from autonomy.

14:41.940 --> 14:43.720
That is such a powerful phrase.

14:44.160 --> 14:46.180
Okay, but there has to be a positive version of this, right?

14:46.340 --> 14:49.360
I mean, not everyone doing a repetitive task is being brainwashed

14:49.360 --> 14:50.820
or trying to numb their exhaustion.

14:51.180 --> 14:52.280
No, absolutely not.

14:52.680 --> 14:55.040
And that is the third group, the competent.

14:55.560 --> 14:56.140
The competent.

14:56.500 --> 14:56.760
Yes.

14:57.240 --> 14:59.800
For them, mechanical activity is practice.

15:00.380 --> 15:04.520
It is the deliberate use of repetition to acquire and hone a high-level skill.

15:04.520 --> 15:08.240
Think of a master carpenter sanding a piece of wood for hours

15:08.240 --> 15:11.340
or a concert pianist playing scales over and over.

15:12.100 --> 15:18.360
So for the competent, the routine actually employs the exercise of choice in pursuit of mastery

15:18.360 --> 15:19.740
rather than just escaping it.

15:19.900 --> 15:20.340
Exactly.

15:20.780 --> 15:25.900
Now, here is the real danger, the central trap that the author points out.

15:26.460 --> 15:30.840
The form of the activity completely obscures its function.

15:31.380 --> 15:32.480
Wait, what do you mean by that?

15:32.760 --> 15:35.960
I mean, you cannot look at a person's hands while they are working

15:35.960 --> 15:39.740
and know which of the three experiences they are having inside their mind.

15:40.140 --> 15:40.800
Oh, wow.

15:41.260 --> 15:44.680
Are they a young person having their disposition violently altered?

15:44.680 --> 15:49.280
Are they an exhausted worker finding relief by turning off their decision-making apparatus?

15:49.920 --> 15:52.120
Or are they a master refining their craft?

15:52.820 --> 15:54.760
The physical output looks identical.

15:55.380 --> 15:56.080
That's wild.

15:56.080 --> 16:02.400
If I can't tell what mode I'm in just by looking at what I'm doing, how do I evaluate my own life?

16:02.840 --> 16:07.740
Like, if I spend my weekend meticulously organizing my garage, am I building character?

16:08.340 --> 16:11.120
Am I hiding from the stress of my job by turning my brain off?

16:11.200 --> 16:15.240
Or am I genuinely trying to become a master of garage organization?

16:15.740 --> 16:17.860
It is the essential diagnostic question.

16:18.260 --> 16:22.740
And to help us answer it, the paper pivots to a surprisingly modern technical framework.

16:22.800 --> 16:23.840
Yeah, this part was super cool.

16:23.840 --> 16:26.800
To really understand how this repetition affects our minds,

16:27.220 --> 16:31.100
and especially to distinguish between the exhausted person seeking relief

16:31.100 --> 16:33.420
and the competent person building skill,

16:33.980 --> 16:36.500
the author uses concepts from software engineering.

16:36.780 --> 16:39.240
I found this transition so fascinating.

16:39.720 --> 16:43.180
Taking concepts meant for computer code and applying them to human psychology.

16:43.400 --> 16:44.360
It's a very clever move.

16:44.540 --> 16:48.540
Yeah, the paper references computer scientists David Pernice and John Hughes,

16:49.080 --> 16:53.300
specifically looking at their foundational work on modularity in software design.

16:53.300 --> 16:55.640
It is a highly effective analogy.

16:56.400 --> 17:00.080
In software engineering, a module is a self-contained piece of code.

17:00.840 --> 17:06.200
A module is considered valuable and well-designed if it hides its internal complex decisions

17:06.200 --> 17:08.800
behind a very simple, narrow interface.

17:08.940 --> 17:09.100
Okay.

17:09.680 --> 17:13.740
The philosophy here is that if a specific function reliably produces a result

17:13.740 --> 17:15.780
based only on the inputs you give it,

17:16.320 --> 17:19.020
you can use it, test it, and reuse it

17:19.020 --> 17:22.920
without ever needing to understand the massive, complicated program surrounding it.

17:23.120 --> 17:24.800
So it's basically like a calculator.

17:25.140 --> 17:27.580
You punch in the numbers, you hit equals, and you get the answer.

17:27.780 --> 17:32.000
You don't need to know how the microchip is routing electricity to trust the result.

17:32.240 --> 17:32.760
Precisely.

17:32.760 --> 17:37.300
The paper suggests that human practice, the repetition of a task,

17:37.640 --> 17:39.800
does something very similar to our cognition.

17:40.600 --> 17:45.340
When you practice a procedure, until you can do it without agonizing over every single step,

17:45.660 --> 17:47.120
it becomes cheap to execute.

17:47.320 --> 17:47.580
Right.

17:47.980 --> 17:50.340
It drastically reduces your cognitive load.

17:50.340 --> 17:57.180
Let's really dig into this, because the paper introduces two distinct terms here that sound similar

17:57.180 --> 18:01.080
but represent opposite outcomes, encapsulation and insulation.

18:01.540 --> 18:02.900
They are critical to separate, yes.

18:02.980 --> 18:03.220
Yes.

18:04.080 --> 18:07.160
Because one is a success mode for the human mind,

18:07.600 --> 18:10.240
and the other is a catastrophic failure mode.

18:10.500 --> 18:12.000
Okay, start with the success mode.

18:12.520 --> 18:14.020
Encapsulation is the success mode.

18:14.020 --> 18:18.820
It means successfully hiding internal decisions behind that narrow interface

18:18.820 --> 18:21.740
so a procedure is reliable and cheap to execute.

18:22.060 --> 18:22.160
Right.

18:22.360 --> 18:28.380
But crucially, a well-encapsulated module, or a well-encapsulated human habit,

18:28.820 --> 18:33.400
remains entirely responsive to explicit inputs or changes in the environment.

18:33.800 --> 18:37.660
Okay, let me try an analogy here to see if I am grasping the practical difference.

18:37.760 --> 18:38.200
Go for it.

18:38.380 --> 18:42.280
Let's talk about driving a car, specifically the feeling of being on autopilot.

18:42.280 --> 18:44.000
And I don't mean a self-driving Tesla.

18:44.120 --> 18:45.280
I mean human brain autopilot.

18:46.100 --> 18:46.420
Oh, yeah.

18:46.740 --> 18:47.920
A very common experience.

18:48.140 --> 18:48.300
Right.

18:48.780 --> 18:52.080
So encapsulation would be when you are driving on the highway,

18:52.500 --> 18:55.840
seamlessly shifting gears, checking your blind spots, maintaining speed.

18:56.140 --> 19:01.120
But you are doing all of this while having a deeply complex emotional conversation with your passenger.

19:01.520 --> 19:01.820
Yes.

19:02.280 --> 19:03.960
The act of driving is encapsulated.

19:04.340 --> 19:05.500
It is cheap to execute.

19:05.960 --> 19:08.860
Your higher order brain is entirely focused on the conversation.

19:09.560 --> 19:11.340
But, and this is the key,

19:11.340 --> 19:15.180
if a deer suddenly jumps into the road, you instantly slam on the brakes.

19:15.320 --> 19:15.540
Right.

19:15.640 --> 19:16.000
Exactly.

19:16.360 --> 19:20.220
The encapsulated routine is still taking relevant inputs from reality.

19:20.480 --> 19:22.820
That is a superb illustration of encapsulation.

19:23.420 --> 19:26.040
Your higher order attention is liberated by the routine,

19:26.340 --> 19:29.480
but the routine itself has not severed its connection to the outside world.

19:29.660 --> 19:30.680
It remains responsive.

19:31.020 --> 19:31.220
Right.

19:31.220 --> 19:34.820
So then insulation, which is the failure mode,

19:34.920 --> 19:38.500
is when you get in your car on a Saturday morning to go to the grocery store.

19:38.960 --> 19:41.520
You totally zone out, and 20 minutes later,

19:41.720 --> 19:44.360
you pull into the parking lot of your old office building,

19:44.580 --> 19:46.800
a place you haven't worked at for three years.

19:46.800 --> 19:49.220
Yes, we've all done something like that.

19:49.300 --> 19:49.560
Right.

19:49.980 --> 19:50.960
You drove perfectly,

19:51.280 --> 19:53.100
you obeyed all the traffic laws,

19:53.200 --> 19:56.280
but you ended up in the wrong place because the routine ran blindly.

19:56.720 --> 19:59.860
It was completely disconnected from the context of what day it was,

19:59.920 --> 20:02.140
and what your actual goal was.

20:02.620 --> 20:03.020
Exactly.

20:03.440 --> 20:04.740
The relevant information,

20:04.940 --> 20:07.040
that it is Saturday and unique groceries,

20:07.180 --> 20:07.940
not spreadsheets,

20:08.140 --> 20:10.800
was entirely blocked from reaching the driving procedure.

20:11.420 --> 20:12.420
It was insulated.

20:12.760 --> 20:13.940
The routine was insulated.

20:13.940 --> 20:17.620
The paper actually uses the example of language learning

20:17.620 --> 20:19.360
to demonstrate this on an academic level.

20:19.380 --> 20:20.140
Oh, right, the drills.

20:20.820 --> 20:22.380
Think about doing grammatical drills.

20:23.140 --> 20:25.400
You repeat a verb conjugation over and over

20:25.400 --> 20:27.880
until you can produce it without conscious thought.

20:28.560 --> 20:30.460
Like, je suis, tu es, il est.

20:30.780 --> 20:31.160
Exactly.

20:31.820 --> 20:33.900
That is lowering the deliberative cost.

20:34.240 --> 20:36.260
It is building procedural reliability.

20:37.180 --> 20:39.900
But contrast that with integrated use,

20:39.900 --> 20:43.760
like actually having a fluid conversation in a café in Paris.

20:44.520 --> 20:46.700
Where you have to actually know when to use the verb

20:46.700 --> 20:49.460
and why you are saying it, not just how to conjugate it?

20:49.700 --> 20:49.980
Yes.

20:50.540 --> 20:52.660
If you only ever experience the drill,

20:53.520 --> 20:55.520
you are at high risk of becoming insulated.

20:56.020 --> 20:58.700
You can produce perfectly correct sentences on a test,

20:59.280 --> 21:01.420
but in reality, you might deploy those sentences

21:01.420 --> 21:03.880
in a way that completely misses the social context

21:03.880 --> 21:05.240
or the point of the conversation.

21:05.480 --> 21:05.740
Right.

21:06.540 --> 21:09.820
Integrated use keeps the cognitive form answerable

21:09.820 --> 21:11.680
to what is actually happening in the world.

21:12.900 --> 21:14.100
Wait, pause for a second.

21:14.420 --> 21:18.700
Because this completely flips the modern mindfulness narrative on its head.

21:18.940 --> 21:19.300
How so?

21:19.520 --> 21:21.300
Well, we are constantly sold this idea

21:21.300 --> 21:24.140
that we need to be hyper-present for every little thing we do.

21:24.260 --> 21:27.140
You know, wash the dishes mindfully, walk mindfully.

21:27.380 --> 21:28.020
Right, right.

21:28.020 --> 21:29.740
But if I am understanding this correctly,

21:29.960 --> 21:32.480
you are saying that if I am hyper-mindful

21:32.480 --> 21:35.140
of exactly how my foot hits the gas pedal while driving,

21:35.600 --> 21:37.400
I am actually restricting my autonomy

21:37.400 --> 21:40.640
because my brain has no bandwidth left for higher-order thinking.

21:40.640 --> 21:43.600
That is the brilliant psychological stake the paper highlights.

21:44.360 --> 21:46.280
Automaticity and autonomy are not opposites.

21:46.440 --> 21:46.720
They aren't.

21:46.880 --> 21:47.020
No.

21:47.620 --> 21:50.660
Autonomy actually relies on automaticity.

21:51.700 --> 21:54.540
Becoming automatic at a task reduces your cognitive load,

21:54.780 --> 21:57.420
which frees up your judgment to supervise the bigger picture.

21:57.700 --> 21:58.400
Oh, wow.

21:58.700 --> 22:00.080
You cannot compose a symphony

22:00.080 --> 22:03.380
if you have to consciously think about where the C key is on the piano.

22:03.620 --> 22:05.400
That is such a relief to hear, honestly.

22:05.640 --> 22:06.440
It is. It really is.

22:06.840 --> 22:09.260
But the danger, the real threat to your independence,

22:09.260 --> 22:12.780
is when your judgment becomes subordinated to the routine.

22:13.140 --> 22:13.860
When it flips.

22:14.160 --> 22:14.440
Yes.

22:14.740 --> 22:17.060
It's when the attention that is freed up by the repetition

22:17.060 --> 22:20.180
isn't redirected toward higher-order thinking or supervision,

22:20.400 --> 22:24.500
but is just captured by yet another unexamined insulated routine.

22:24.820 --> 22:27.700
Okay, so if our routines can run on autopilot,

22:28.280 --> 22:29.980
heavily insulated from the outside world,

22:30.120 --> 22:32.360
it raises a really terrifying question.

22:32.560 --> 22:32.940
Which is?

22:33.300 --> 22:36.200
When I accidentally drive to my old office on a Saturday,

22:36.200 --> 22:38.580
who programmed that destination into my head?

22:38.580 --> 22:42.320
If I am not consciously steering, whose map am I following?

22:42.920 --> 22:44.060
That is the million-dollar question.

22:44.660 --> 22:47.920
This brings us to the very core of the paper and the titular concept,

22:48.320 --> 22:49.520
the non-player character.

22:49.800 --> 22:51.720
But to understand how we become NPCs,

22:51.880 --> 22:53.880
the author says we first have to break down

22:53.880 --> 22:56.520
how a purpose is actually formed.

22:56.900 --> 22:59.420
They call this the three achievements of purpose.

22:59.680 --> 23:02.540
Yes, and the text does a masterful job of untangling this,

23:02.880 --> 23:05.120
because we often mash a few different concepts together

23:05.120 --> 23:08.000
when we talk about being the author of our own lives.

23:08.000 --> 23:09.660
Right, we just say, I did it.

23:10.100 --> 23:10.460
Exactly.

23:11.240 --> 23:14.780
But the first achievement the paper lists is originating a purpose.

23:15.460 --> 23:18.360
This means being the absolute first source of an idea.

23:18.560 --> 23:21.400
Like inventing a completely new concept out of thin air.

23:21.600 --> 23:21.900
Correct.

23:22.280 --> 23:27.060
And the paper quickly points out that demanding this level of origination is a fool's errand.

23:27.420 --> 23:28.500
It is nearly impossible.

23:28.840 --> 23:29.140
Why?

23:29.140 --> 23:33.680
Because almost all human purposes are inherited from our culture,

23:34.000 --> 23:35.420
our parents, our environment.

23:36.260 --> 23:40.300
If your purpose is to cure a disease or play the cello or land on Mars,

23:40.580 --> 23:44.580
you are taking up a goal that society constructed long before you were born.

23:44.660 --> 23:44.780
Right.

23:44.840 --> 23:45.860
You didn't invent Mars.

23:46.040 --> 23:46.400
Exactly.

23:47.060 --> 23:50.320
The paper argues that demanding complete origination

23:50.320 --> 23:53.240
would convict everyone of being a drone,

23:53.460 --> 23:55.080
even the greatest historical figures.

23:55.080 --> 23:58.460
But if originating a purpose is impossible

23:58.460 --> 24:00.880
because we inherit all our goals from culture,

24:00.980 --> 24:03.440
doesn't that doom us all to NPC status immediately?

24:03.800 --> 24:04.960
Is there no way out?

24:05.160 --> 24:05.860
There is a way out.

24:06.140 --> 24:08.420
And it lies in the subsequent achievements.

24:08.800 --> 24:11.080
Since we cannot originate most things,

24:11.460 --> 24:14.440
the second achievement is endorsing a purpose.

24:15.020 --> 24:15.580
Endorsing.

24:15.640 --> 24:15.860
Mm-hmm.

24:16.180 --> 24:16.480
Okay.

24:16.820 --> 24:20.780
This means taking up a purpose after genuinely reflecting on it,

24:20.780 --> 24:24.060
regardless of where the idea originally came from.

24:24.060 --> 24:25.280
To explain this,

24:25.440 --> 24:28.220
the paper leans heavily on the philosopher Harry Frankfurt

24:28.220 --> 24:31.560
and his distinction between wantons and persons.

24:31.740 --> 24:32.900
Oh, I love this concept.

24:33.060 --> 24:33.280
Mm-hmm.

24:33.760 --> 24:37.840
A wanton isn't just someone draped over a couch eating grapes, right?

24:37.920 --> 24:40.640
It has a very specific philosophical definition here.

24:40.820 --> 24:41.220
It does.

24:41.580 --> 24:42.740
In Frankfurt's terminology,

24:42.920 --> 24:46.020
a wanton is someone who possesses desires and acts on them,

24:46.460 --> 24:48.100
sometimes with incredible skill and efficiency,

24:48.100 --> 24:51.240
but they simply do not care which of their desires prevails.

24:51.620 --> 24:52.940
So they don't judge their own desires.

24:53.120 --> 24:53.420
Exactly.

24:54.060 --> 24:56.980
They lack a second-order attitude about their own wants.

24:57.460 --> 25:00.160
They just operate on whatever urge is loudest in the moment.

25:00.940 --> 25:02.360
A person, in Frankfurt's view,

25:02.440 --> 25:04.700
has the capacity to look at their conflicting desires,

25:05.220 --> 25:06.140
reflect on them, and say,

25:06.720 --> 25:09.880
I identify with this desire, but I actively reject that one.

25:10.160 --> 25:11.360
Ah, got it.

25:11.740 --> 25:14.180
That reflective identification is endorsement.

25:14.740 --> 25:17.680
So if you are listening to this on your commute, ask yourself,

25:17.860 --> 25:20.520
when you grab a coffee you don't even really want,

25:20.520 --> 25:23.000
just because you walked past a Starbucks and smelled the roast,

25:23.500 --> 25:26.820
are you operating as a wanton or a person in that exact moment?

25:26.820 --> 25:27.660
That's a great test.

25:27.660 --> 25:32.960
The wanton part of you is just executing the immediate desire for caffeine and sugar.

25:33.180 --> 25:36.480
The person part of you is the internal voice that might say,

25:36.640 --> 25:40.540
I really wish I didn't want to spend $6 on this right now.

25:40.700 --> 25:41.940
I'm trying to save money.

25:42.180 --> 25:45.060
That is a perfect everyday application of Frankfurt's theory.

25:45.760 --> 25:51.080
And that internal conflict leads directly to the third and most crucial achievement of purpose,

25:51.080 --> 25:56.940
revising, revising, so originating, endorsing, and now revising.

25:57.120 --> 25:57.260
Yes.

25:57.660 --> 26:02.240
Revising is the capacity to reconsider the fundamental relationship between your purpose

26:02.240 --> 26:05.680
and your activity when the circumstances inevitably change.

26:06.300 --> 26:10.920
The text brings in Immanuel Kant here, specifically his concept of immaturity.

26:11.480 --> 26:17.820
Kant defines immaturity as the inability to use one's own understanding without the guidance of another, right?

26:18.060 --> 26:19.220
Yes, exactly.

26:19.220 --> 26:25.320
So, true maturity, true authorship of your life, hinges on this third achievement.

26:26.000 --> 26:30.460
It is remaining constantly able to revise your purpose when the evidence demands it.

26:31.080 --> 26:36.840
And this is exactly where the paper formally introduces the concept of the non-player character, the NPC.

26:37.300 --> 26:38.100
Yes, it is.

26:38.340 --> 26:40.520
For anyone unfamiliar with gaming terminology,

26:40.780 --> 26:45.680
an NPC is a character in a video game that is controlled by the game's code, not by a human player.

26:45.680 --> 26:52.340
But the paper is very careful to clarify that being an NPC isn't about being simple or stupid, right?

26:52.460 --> 26:53.260
Incredibly careful.

26:53.660 --> 26:57.840
It states that behavioral richness does not separate the NPC from the protagonist.

26:58.280 --> 27:00.280
Right, because games are so complex now.

27:00.440 --> 27:00.760
Exactly.

27:00.760 --> 27:05.960
In modern, highly advanced games, NPCs have staggering complexity.

27:06.460 --> 27:07.360
They have daily schedules.

27:07.640 --> 27:09.680
They react dynamically to digital weather.

27:10.040 --> 27:11.640
They remember past interactions.

27:11.780 --> 27:12.900
They even run economies.

27:13.240 --> 27:13.500
Right.

27:13.580 --> 27:17.940
You can have an NPC with a more complex daily routine than the actual human playing the game.

27:18.140 --> 27:18.420
Totally.

27:18.860 --> 27:21.740
So if it isn't complexity that defines them, what does?

27:21.740 --> 27:24.920
What defines an NPC is structural dependence.

27:25.500 --> 27:29.980
The objectives of the NPC are specified entirely from the outside by the game designer.

27:30.400 --> 27:35.040
And those core objectives are simply not available to the NPC for revision.

27:35.320 --> 27:35.540
Right.

27:35.820 --> 27:39.000
The digital blacksmith doesn't wake up one day and decide to become a poet.

27:39.800 --> 27:41.340
Keeping the forge is not a choice.

27:41.600 --> 27:44.240
Keeping the forge is what the character is for.

27:44.720 --> 27:44.980
Wow.

27:45.500 --> 27:48.120
This is where the paper's thesis hits like a ton of bricks.

27:48.380 --> 27:49.060
It really does.

27:49.060 --> 27:53.540
Think back to the expert gamer who grinds for a month to earn a rare digital sword.

27:54.020 --> 27:58.200
That human player is making hundreds of complex, tactical choices every minute.

27:58.680 --> 28:03.340
They are optimizing their route, managing limited resources, calculating probabilities.

28:03.760 --> 28:06.360
They're exercising immense cognitive skill.

28:06.700 --> 28:06.960
Yes.

28:07.660 --> 28:12.880
But they are doing all of it to fulfill a loop and unlock a reward that was entirely conceived

28:12.880 --> 28:14.580
and structured by the game designer.

28:14.580 --> 28:19.660
In that dynamic, the highly skilled human gamer is actually the NPC in relation to the game

28:19.660 --> 28:20.020
designer.

28:20.480 --> 28:22.000
It is a profound inversion.

28:22.380 --> 28:27.480
You can possess an enormous repertoire of highly competent, wildly complex behaviors while

28:27.480 --> 28:29.560
remaining completely structurally dependent.

28:30.020 --> 28:31.900
Because you can't revise the core purpose.

28:32.040 --> 28:32.360
Exactly.

28:33.000 --> 28:38.460
If your purposes are supplied without examination by convention, by your employer, by your educational

28:38.460 --> 28:42.560
institution, or by a game designer, you are operating as an NPC.

28:42.560 --> 28:44.260
The system is the protagonist.

28:44.540 --> 28:44.860
Exactly.

28:45.780 --> 28:51.580
The paper links this back to Nietzsche's aphorism 886, where he controversially states that nothing

28:51.580 --> 28:53.700
is more rare than a personal act.

28:54.040 --> 28:56.640
Which, on the surface, I mean, that sounds incredibly arrogant.

28:57.000 --> 28:58.200
Oh, look at the herd.

28:58.540 --> 29:00.320
No one does anything original except me.

29:00.720 --> 29:02.060
It definitely has that sting.

29:02.480 --> 29:08.220
But the paper argues that Nietzsche is making a structural diagnostic claim, not merely an elitist

29:08.220 --> 29:08.660
insult.

29:08.940 --> 29:09.680
Okay, explain that.

29:09.680 --> 29:15.740
When he says that most human acts express class, rank, or environment rather than a true

29:15.740 --> 29:20.980
personality, he means that most of what we do all day is merely discharging a role that

29:20.980 --> 29:21.960
was handed to us.

29:22.240 --> 29:23.300
Just playing our part.

29:23.600 --> 29:23.880
Right.

29:25.000 --> 29:30.920
A true personality, in this specific philosophical sense, is a human being who has achieved the

29:30.920 --> 29:35.660
monumental task of organizing their drives under a coherent direction that they have actually

29:35.660 --> 29:39.200
examined, endorsed, and remained willing to revise.

29:39.680 --> 29:43.780
So, building on that, Nietzsche takes it a step further with his description of the, quote,

29:44.080 --> 29:47.360
virtuous man, end quote, from aphorism 319.

29:47.980 --> 29:48.540
Oh, yes.

29:48.980 --> 29:50.440
This part is intense.

29:50.660 --> 29:51.200
It is.

29:51.560 --> 29:56.800
He says the virtuous man is actually a lower species because his entire value comes from

29:56.800 --> 29:58.960
conforming perfectly to a fixed scheme.

29:58.960 --> 30:04.260
He is literally the missing puzzle piece that fits flawlessly into the whole society carved

30:04.260 --> 30:04.820
out for him.

30:05.440 --> 30:10.320
But because his value is conferred entirely by the scheme itself, and not by anything he

30:10.320 --> 30:14.140
authored or supervised, he is structurally an NPC.

30:14.620 --> 30:14.980
Exactly.

30:15.440 --> 30:19.840
And we really must emphasize how forcefully the paper rejects the modern internet slang use

30:19.840 --> 30:20.500
of NPC.

30:20.720 --> 30:21.860
Yes, this is so important.

30:21.860 --> 30:27.240
Online people use NPC to dehumanize anyone who disagrees with them politically, or anyone

30:27.240 --> 30:30.740
who follows a mainstream trend, implying they are just brainless sheep.

30:31.100 --> 30:32.400
It's a really cheap insult.

30:32.660 --> 30:33.040
It is.

30:33.640 --> 30:36.000
The author reclaims the term from this toxic usage.

30:36.640 --> 30:40.800
It is not an insult about your intelligence, your emotional depth, or your political affiliation.

30:41.140 --> 30:44.920
It is a structural diagnostic of how your purposes are organized.

30:45.340 --> 30:48.600
So, you could be a genius and still be an NPC.

30:48.900 --> 30:49.280
Absolutely.

30:49.280 --> 30:54.760
You could be a brilliantly intelligent surgeon, executing unimaginably complex, life-saving

30:54.760 --> 30:55.220
tasks.

30:55.460 --> 31:00.120
But if you have never examined or endorsed why you are in that operating room, you are

31:00.120 --> 31:02.420
operating as an NPC within the medical system.

31:02.780 --> 31:03.040
Wow.

31:03.420 --> 31:08.000
The system handed you the purpose, and you are just an incredibly high-functioning executor.

31:08.220 --> 31:14.740
So, if being an NPC isn't about simple behavior, and being a protagonist isn't just about coming

31:14.740 --> 31:20.140
up with completely original ideas, how do we actually measure true independence?

31:20.340 --> 31:23.420
What is the test to see if I am the author of my life?

31:23.420 --> 31:27.620
The paper proposes a very specific criterion to measure this.

31:28.700 --> 31:30.500
Responsiveness to reasons.

31:31.500 --> 31:33.060
Responsiveness to reasons.

31:33.600 --> 31:33.860
Yes.

31:34.080 --> 31:38.720
And it immediately establishes a vital philosophical distinction to explain how this works.

31:39.200 --> 31:42.320
Responsiveness is modal, not occurrent.

31:42.960 --> 31:43.540
Okay.

31:43.680 --> 31:45.500
I'm going to need you to unpack those terms.

31:45.680 --> 31:47.080
Modal versus occurrent.

31:47.560 --> 31:48.060
Absolutely.

31:48.060 --> 31:53.140
Occurrent refers to what is happening right now, in the physical present moment.

31:53.360 --> 31:56.160
It is the behavior we can physically observe.

31:56.420 --> 31:57.880
Like me talking right now.

31:58.340 --> 31:58.700
Exactly.

31:59.440 --> 32:04.060
The paper argues that if we judge someone's independence based solely on their recurrent

32:04.060 --> 32:06.240
behavior, we will almost certainly get it wrong.

32:06.680 --> 32:07.080
Why?

32:07.560 --> 32:08.980
It gives a fantastic example.

32:09.500 --> 32:14.220
A very tempting way to define an independent thinker is to say, the independent person is

32:14.220 --> 32:18.600
the one who boldly quits their routine the minute the external reward disappears.

32:19.420 --> 32:19.960
Oh, right.

32:20.040 --> 32:23.100
Like, I stopped doing the corporate grind the second they cut my bonus.

32:23.320 --> 32:24.480
I am a free thinker.

32:24.620 --> 32:24.980
Exactly.

32:25.460 --> 32:29.940
But the paper points out that this is far too simple, and often entirely backwards.

32:30.880 --> 32:35.640
Someone who instantly abandons a project the moment the external rewards vanish might

32:35.640 --> 32:40.900
just be demonstrating their complete and utter dependence on those external rewards.

32:40.900 --> 32:41.620
Oh, I see.

32:42.300 --> 32:43.160
They aren't independent.

32:43.300 --> 32:44.400
They're just mercenaries.

32:44.640 --> 32:44.760
Right.

32:45.340 --> 32:47.980
Their actions are still entirely dictated by the system's ledger.

32:48.920 --> 32:54.100
Conversely, someone who stubbornly persists in a routine, even after the apparent immediate

32:54.100 --> 32:59.180
purpose is gone, might be doing so for a very profound independent reason.

32:59.780 --> 33:00.320
Like what?

33:00.720 --> 33:03.140
Well, they might be trying to preserve a long-term possibility.

33:03.540 --> 33:08.500
Or they might be actively rejecting an institutional judgment that the project is worthless.

33:08.500 --> 33:11.840
You simply cannot know by looking at the occurrent behavior.

33:13.060 --> 33:14.780
So that is where modal comes in.

33:15.100 --> 33:17.560
Modal is about potentiality.

33:17.920 --> 33:20.900
It's about what would happen if the variables changed.

33:21.700 --> 33:22.220
Precisely.

33:22.860 --> 33:27.440
It is about how the agent would respond when the underlying circumstances that support their

33:27.440 --> 33:28.580
activity shift.

33:28.960 --> 33:31.620
The operational definition of the paper provides is this.

33:31.620 --> 33:38.600
Responsiveness is a demonstrated sensitivity to relevant changes in evidence, in consequences,

33:39.320 --> 33:40.980
and in available alternatives.

33:41.980 --> 33:46.760
So going back to the software analogy from earlier, it is about keeping your interfaces

33:46.760 --> 33:50.740
open to new inputs from reality rather than insulating your routine.

33:51.080 --> 33:52.340
Yes, exactly.

33:52.840 --> 33:57.560
And what is truly fascinating here is how the paper distinguishes between genuine responsiveness

33:57.560 --> 33:59.400
and mere rationalization.

33:59.460 --> 34:00.180
Oh, rationalization.

34:00.180 --> 34:05.220
Yes, this is a trap that highly intelligent people fall into all the time.

34:06.100 --> 34:11.320
An agent can produce incredibly elaborate, logically tight, highly articulate justifications

34:11.320 --> 34:16.660
for continuing an activity, even when all the empirical evidence screams that they should

34:16.660 --> 34:17.000
stop.

34:17.480 --> 34:18.880
Oh, we all have a friend like this.

34:18.920 --> 34:21.220
Or, honestly, we've been this person.

34:21.780 --> 34:27.300
You are in a toxic job, or a doomed relationship, or a creative project that is clearly failing.

34:27.300 --> 34:32.100
And if someone asks you why you are still pouring your soul into it, you don't just shrug.

34:32.260 --> 34:33.160
No, you explain it.

34:33.340 --> 34:33.500
Yeah.

34:33.580 --> 34:38.120
You give them a 20-minute, highly intellectualized dissertation on why staying is actually the

34:38.120 --> 34:40.520
most strategic, virtuous move possible.

34:40.880 --> 34:44.860
That is the perfect example of insulation disguised as intellect.

34:45.540 --> 34:50.980
The routine is entirely insulated from reality, and the intellect has merely been hired as a PR

34:50.980 --> 34:52.400
firm to defend the routine.

34:52.400 --> 34:54.960
A PR firm for your bad habits.

34:55.120 --> 34:55.460
Exactly.

34:56.420 --> 35:01.000
True responsiveness means that your actual behavior would genuinely change, whether that

35:01.000 --> 35:05.740
means slightly modifying the activity, completely abandoning it, or even doubling down on it

35:05.740 --> 35:10.660
with a new strategy based on actual shifts in evidence or consequences, not just spinning

35:10.660 --> 35:11.320
a new narrative.

35:11.700 --> 35:13.560
But wait, I have a logical issue with this.

35:13.640 --> 35:14.140
Let's hear it.

35:14.140 --> 35:19.460
How do I, or anyone else, know what changes are considered, quote, relevant?

35:19.860 --> 35:24.640
Like, if I decide to completely ignore a piece of negative feedback on a project, maybe I am

35:24.640 --> 35:26.040
just insulating myself in denial.

35:26.240 --> 35:26.360
Sure.

35:26.620 --> 35:33.020
But maybe I actually have a very sound, independent reason to believe that specific feedback is

35:33.020 --> 35:33.980
irrelevant noise.

35:33.980 --> 35:41.740
Isn't this whole definition, judging if someone is sensitive to relevant evidence, completely

35:41.740 --> 35:42.220
circular?

35:42.740 --> 35:46.760
It is an excellent critique, and the paper actually embraces it.

35:47.360 --> 35:47.680
Really?

35:47.980 --> 35:48.280
Yes.

35:49.120 --> 35:54.280
The author writes, the criterion retains a residual circularity that should be owned.

35:55.320 --> 36:01.200
Determining which specific changes count as relevant is the exact messy human process that

36:01.200 --> 36:03.300
responsiveness is supposed to settle.

36:03.980 --> 36:05.420
I actually appreciate that.

36:05.940 --> 36:10.300
It's a refreshingly honest academic move to just admit, yes, there is a loop here.

36:10.720 --> 36:13.160
Human psychology isn't a neat math equation.

36:13.600 --> 36:14.920
It is necessary honesty.

36:15.700 --> 36:19.900
The text clarifies that responsiveness is what philosophers call a virtue term.

36:20.520 --> 36:24.780
It's not a neat, predictive algorithm you can run in advance to guarantee an outcome.

36:25.360 --> 36:26.900
You can't just plug in the variables.

36:27.620 --> 36:29.420
It organizes retrospective judgments.

36:29.420 --> 36:34.380
We generally only know if someone was truly responsive to reasons by looking back at their

36:34.380 --> 36:38.840
track record over time, seeing how they navigated complex shifts in their environment.

36:39.280 --> 36:40.100
That makes a lot of sense.

36:40.600 --> 36:46.840
To further clarify this, the paper brings in a vital nuance from Nietzsche's aphorism 886C

36:46.840 --> 36:50.300
regarding what he calls selective permeability.

36:50.300 --> 36:53.700
What does selective permeability look like in practice?

36:54.160 --> 36:57.460
Nietzsche describes a true personality as a walled enclosure.

36:57.920 --> 36:58.220
He says,

36:58.700 --> 37:03.280
A strong individual is capable of shutting out the world and maintaining a much lower degree

37:03.280 --> 37:08.160
of sensitiveness than the average person, because the average person is too easily infected

37:08.160 --> 37:08.740
by the herd.

37:09.160 --> 37:11.080
Okay, but wait, that sounds like a contradiction.

37:11.540 --> 37:15.820
Shutting out the world sounds like the exact opposite of keeping your interfaces open.

37:15.820 --> 37:19.080
It seems like a contradiction, until you look at the mechanism.

37:19.940 --> 37:24.580
Responsiveness is a high sensitivity to what actually bears on your specific purposes.

37:25.340 --> 37:28.720
Hard evidence, real consequences, viable alternatives.

37:29.100 --> 37:29.380
Okay.

37:29.780 --> 37:34.220
It is not a susceptibility to the random, fluctuating opinions of everyone around you.

37:34.860 --> 37:38.460
A well-bounded, autonomous agent is selectively permeable.

37:38.640 --> 37:41.160
So they let the good stuff in and keep the noise out.

37:41.440 --> 37:41.880
Exactly.

37:41.880 --> 37:48.060
They are solidly closed to social contagion, peer pressure, and herd mentality, but they

37:48.060 --> 37:51.620
remain completely open and vulnerable to relevant evidence.

37:52.600 --> 37:57.520
Isolation is a failure of the boundary in one direction, blocking out all reality.

37:58.520 --> 38:03.220
Susceptibility is a failure in the opposite direction, having no walls at all and letting

38:03.220 --> 38:05.420
every random trend dictate your actions.

38:06.040 --> 38:07.960
Okay, that makes total sense now.

38:07.960 --> 38:14.440
So we have established how the individual mind handles routines and how a person manages

38:14.440 --> 38:16.820
evidence to avoid becoming an NPC.

38:17.300 --> 38:17.340
Right.

38:17.560 --> 38:19.720
But this raises a terrifying problem.

38:20.380 --> 38:25.800
It's one thing to say an individual can be responsive to reasons and maintain their internal

38:25.800 --> 38:26.280
autonomy.

38:26.840 --> 38:32.960
But what happens when that individual walks into a modern corporation or a massive university?

38:33.420 --> 38:34.920
That's where things get complicated.

38:34.920 --> 38:40.220
Yeah, because a business doesn't necessarily want autonomous thinkers constantly revising

38:40.220 --> 38:40.760
their purposes.

38:41.280 --> 38:44.720
It wants reliable, predictable, interchangeable parts.

38:45.260 --> 38:49.880
This brings us to how society actually values our work, which the paper calls the logic of

38:49.880 --> 38:50.440
exchange.

38:50.860 --> 38:56.440
This is where the structural critique moves from psychology to sociology and becomes very sharp.

38:56.440 --> 39:02.860
The paper looks closely at Nietzsche's intense rejection of requital, which is the idea of

39:02.860 --> 39:07.780
payback or moral equivalence, found in aphorisms 925 and 926.

39:08.780 --> 39:09.220
Okay.

39:09.540 --> 39:15.000
In these notes, Nietzsche strongly critiques both the golden rule and the utilitarian philosophy

39:15.000 --> 39:15.940
of John Stuart Mill.

39:16.140 --> 39:17.220
Wait, the golden rule?

39:17.500 --> 39:19.880
Do you want to others as you would have them do unto you?

39:19.980 --> 39:23.180
That is the bedrock of most ethical systems.

39:23.340 --> 39:25.360
Why on earth would he critique that?

39:25.360 --> 39:31.360
Because Nietzsche argues that the golden rule and the entire modern ethos of mutual services

39:31.360 --> 39:37.500
and fair trade presupposes that human actions are invariably requited, meaning they can be

39:37.500 --> 39:38.760
measured and paid back equally.

39:39.180 --> 39:40.420
Like a one-to-one exchange.

39:40.720 --> 39:40.920
Right.

39:41.440 --> 39:44.300
It assumes a fundamental equivalence between actions.

39:45.040 --> 39:46.800
Nietzsche vehemently denies this.

39:47.420 --> 39:53.820
He argues that among real, distinct, complex individuals, equal actions simply do not exist.

39:53.820 --> 39:55.780
Because everyone is different.

39:56.040 --> 39:56.320
Exactly.

39:57.100 --> 39:58.860
Every act is uniquely situated.

39:59.660 --> 40:01.740
Therefore, true requital is impossible.

40:02.540 --> 40:08.080
The hypothesis of equivalence is, in his words, ignoble, because it systematically cancels

40:08.080 --> 40:12.840
out and ignores the part of a human action that has no equivalent, the part that cannot

40:12.840 --> 40:15.980
be measured on a scale and remunerated with a paycheck.

40:15.980 --> 40:21.940
So, what the paper is extracting from this isn't necessarily Nietzsche's moral outrage,

40:22.040 --> 40:24.540
but the structural assumption lurking underneath it.

40:24.900 --> 40:25.380
Precisely.

40:25.700 --> 40:30.580
To operate a massive system of equivalence to be able to say, this specific action equals

40:30.580 --> 40:35.440
exactly this much reward, you have to assume that the people performing the actions are

40:35.440 --> 40:36.700
completely interchangeable.

40:36.700 --> 40:37.420
Exactly.

40:38.080 --> 40:40.660
This is the linchpin of the institutional critique.

40:41.000 --> 40:43.880
The paper uses the term fungibility.

40:44.340 --> 40:45.220
Fungibility, right.

40:45.320 --> 40:46.280
Like a dollar bill.

40:47.060 --> 40:47.440
Yes.

40:47.760 --> 40:54.400
If an institution uses a system of equivalence, like hourly wages or standardized test scores,

40:54.820 --> 40:58.920
it fundamentally requires interchangeable fungible role occupants.

40:59.440 --> 41:01.660
Think back to our software analogy from earlier.

41:01.940 --> 41:02.660
With the modules.

41:02.660 --> 41:03.220
Yes.

41:04.500 --> 41:07.760
A module is defined entirely by its interface.

41:08.460 --> 41:13.420
Any piece of code that successfully meets the input-output specification can replace any

41:13.420 --> 41:16.080
other piece of code, and the system won't care at all.

41:16.720 --> 41:20.540
A role-bound human executor is designed to be just as fungible.

41:21.420 --> 41:23.240
So, treating a worker as fungible?

41:23.560 --> 41:27.140
I mean, it's basically treating humans like a USB port on a computer.

41:27.460 --> 41:28.340
A USB port.

41:28.440 --> 41:29.080
I love that.

41:29.220 --> 41:29.340
Yeah.

41:29.340 --> 41:34.620
Yeah, like the corporation doesn't care if a mouse or a keyboard or a cooling fan is plugged

41:34.620 --> 41:35.340
into the desk.

41:35.620 --> 41:37.880
It just wants the baseline electrical input.

41:38.200 --> 41:42.900
It completely strips away the identity and the unique capabilities of the device in favor

41:42.900 --> 41:44.320
of universal compatibility.

41:44.820 --> 41:46.840
That is a phenomenal analogy.

41:46.960 --> 41:48.140
That is a phenomenal analogy.

41:48.140 --> 41:52.520
The USB port is the ultimate interface of fungibility.

41:53.520 --> 42:01.320
And that paper argues that the grind, whether you are grinding for loot in a game, grinding

42:01.320 --> 42:06.720
for an A in organic chemistry, or grinding for a promotion at a law firm, is essentially

42:06.720 --> 42:09.880
just a massive requital machine.

42:10.200 --> 42:10.880
Requital machine?

42:11.120 --> 42:12.040
A requital machine.

42:12.240 --> 42:12.440
Yeah.

42:13.000 --> 42:14.000
It converts...

42:14.000 --> 42:19.000
So, it's a thing that converts unique human time and effort into a standardized metric.

42:19.000 --> 42:21.860
It converts your life into standing on a ledger.

42:22.560 --> 42:27.420
A university credential, a corporate rank, a credit score, or digital experience points.

42:27.420 --> 42:29.460
These are all equivalence ledgers.

42:30.160 --> 42:32.420
And they only work if we're all the same.

42:32.700 --> 42:33.120
Exactly.

42:33.540 --> 42:38.400
They can only function if the efforts of the people are rendered comparable, and the performers

42:38.400 --> 42:39.680
are rendered interchangeable.

42:40.120 --> 42:44.840
The meter governs the grind, and the meter is the specific device that forces your unique

42:44.840 --> 42:47.960
contribution to be exactly equal to anyone else's.

42:47.960 --> 42:54.200
So, how does an individual using this responsiveness to reasons break out of the fungibility trap?

42:54.620 --> 42:59.040
If I am plugged into the USB port of my company, how do I retain my autonomy?

42:59.580 --> 43:04.300
The paper argues that the independent agent, the one who is truly responsive to reasons,

43:04.560 --> 43:07.700
has the capacity to offer a non-fungible contribution.

43:08.040 --> 43:08.500
Okay, how?

43:08.920 --> 43:13.960
Because they actively supervise their routine and keep their interfaces open to relevant evidence,

43:13.960 --> 43:16.640
they can actually revise the terms of performance.

43:16.640 --> 43:21.680
They don't just blindly satisfy the baseline requirements of the role, they alter what

43:21.680 --> 43:25.400
counts as the performance itself based on what the situation actually needs.

43:26.260 --> 43:26.560
I see.

43:26.880 --> 43:32.740
So, if we bring this directly to the listener's workplace right now, ask yourself, are you just

43:32.740 --> 43:37.760
a USB device that can be unplugged and replaced by any other person with the exact same resume?

43:38.000 --> 43:39.240
It's a tough question to face.

43:39.440 --> 43:39.840
It is.

43:40.240 --> 43:44.940
Or do you interact with your role, your clients, and your problems in a way that the rigid job

43:44.940 --> 43:47.460
description could never capture or demand?

43:47.760 --> 43:47.920
Right.

43:48.200 --> 43:53.080
If your entire focus is just hitting the arbitrary metrics on your annual performance review,

43:53.080 --> 43:59.060
like if your only goal is filling the ledger, you are actively treating yourself as fungible.

43:59.900 --> 44:02.240
That is a vital translation of the concept.

44:02.980 --> 44:08.060
The paper is careful to note that you do not need to be some wildly eccentric genius constantly

44:08.060 --> 44:10.400
being idiosyncratic to be autonomous.

44:10.780 --> 44:11.860
You don't have to be a rebel.

44:11.860 --> 44:12.280
Right.

44:12.380 --> 44:12.720
You don't.

44:13.380 --> 44:18.240
You just need the ongoing capacity to notice when the rigid role is failing to serve its

44:18.240 --> 44:22.160
actual purpose and the ability to revise your contribution accordingly.

44:22.660 --> 44:27.260
What the corporate ledger cannot record is your capacity to alter the ledger itself.

44:27.820 --> 44:29.300
This is a brilliant framework.

44:29.640 --> 44:29.800
Yeah.

44:30.380 --> 44:32.400
But let's make it completely concrete.

44:32.720 --> 44:33.180
Let's do it.

44:33.180 --> 44:38.680
Because we have been spending a lot of time talking about the internal state of the agent,

44:39.000 --> 44:42.220
how we relate to our purposes, how we handle evidence.

44:42.700 --> 44:47.080
But what about the actual physical reality of what we accomplish in the world?

44:47.240 --> 44:47.460
Right.

44:47.600 --> 44:48.080
The output.

44:48.080 --> 44:54.740
Does being a fully autonomous, responsive person guarantee that you are doing good, valuable work?

44:54.740 --> 44:59.700
The paper addresses this direct challenge by separating the question of internal authorship

44:59.700 --> 45:02.900
from the question of real-world function.

45:04.000 --> 45:10.880
And to do this, it introduces a concept from the anthropologist Terence Deacon called contragrade

45:10.880 --> 45:11.500
change.

45:12.640 --> 45:13.900
Contragrade change.

45:14.060 --> 45:14.240
Mm-hmm.

45:14.400 --> 45:14.620
Okay.

45:14.740 --> 45:19.100
We need to unpack this one deeply because it sounds like a term from a physics textbook.

45:19.100 --> 45:22.820
And it's actually rooted in physics, specifically thermodynamics.

45:23.240 --> 45:24.940
And it's a beautiful metaphor.

45:25.900 --> 45:32.240
Contragrade change is the work required to maintain constraints that support a valued organization

45:32.240 --> 45:34.680
against the natural slide into entropy.

45:35.180 --> 45:36.300
Entropy being disorder.

45:36.600 --> 45:36.860
Yes.

45:37.300 --> 45:38.180
Think of a city.

45:38.820 --> 45:45.260
The natural, unmanaged state of a densely populated city is decay, disease, and chaos.

45:45.260 --> 45:51.120
A sanitation worker driving a garbage truck every morning is performing contragrade change.

45:51.560 --> 45:51.580
Okay.

45:51.720 --> 45:52.060
I see.

45:52.880 --> 45:57.580
They are maintaining the physical constraints, removing the refuse, that keep the complex

45:57.580 --> 45:59.480
organization of the city habitable.

45:59.940 --> 46:03.320
They are literally fighting the universe's tendency toward disorder.

46:03.540 --> 46:04.180
Precisely.

46:05.160 --> 46:10.280
The crucial philosophical point the paper makes here is that a sanitation worker does not need

46:10.280 --> 46:17.040
to have independently invented the concept of public health or deeply philosophically endorse

46:17.040 --> 46:21.880
the metaphysics of sanitation to do work that genuinely preserves the city.

46:22.420 --> 46:22.500
Right.

46:22.800 --> 46:23.980
They can just be doing their job.

46:24.480 --> 46:24.820
Exactly.

46:25.220 --> 46:31.620
An externally assigned, unexamined, highly insulated routine can still perform incredibly valuable

46:31.620 --> 46:33.380
constructive work in the real world.

46:33.780 --> 46:39.180
And conversely, an independently chosen, deeply authored activity can be completely destructive to

46:39.180 --> 46:39.480
the world.

46:39.480 --> 46:40.560
Think of a vandal.

46:40.980 --> 46:41.180
Yes.

46:41.460 --> 46:46.280
A vandal might be highly responsive to reasons, totally autonomous, originating their own

46:46.280 --> 46:50.120
unique purpose to spray paint a beautiful historical building.

46:50.260 --> 46:52.780
But they are destroying value, not creating it.

46:52.980 --> 46:54.380
They are accelerating entropy.

46:54.660 --> 46:55.100
Exactly.

46:55.600 --> 46:58.640
So the paper sets up a grid to help us map human action.

46:59.320 --> 47:02.140
On one axis, you have the agent's relation to the purpose.

47:02.300 --> 47:04.520
Is it self-owned or assigned?

47:04.760 --> 47:04.980
Okay.

47:05.120 --> 47:07.080
On the other axis, you have the real world effect.

47:07.080 --> 47:09.240
Is it constructive or destructive?

47:10.100 --> 47:11.880
This gives us four archetypes.

47:12.560 --> 47:13.140
Let's hear them.

47:13.800 --> 47:18.440
You have the self-owned, constructive person, like a visionary architect designing a sustainable

47:18.440 --> 47:18.900
house.

47:19.280 --> 47:22.780
You have the self-owned, destructive person, the vandal.

47:22.780 --> 47:28.080
You have the assigned, constructive person, the reliable, indispensable sanitation worker.

47:28.080 --> 47:28.880
And the fourth?

47:28.880 --> 47:34.740
And finally, you have the assigned, destructive person, the idle, insulated bureaucrat who blindly

47:34.740 --> 47:40.040
pushes papers that maintain nothing but the bloated bureaucracy itself, actively hindering

47:40.040 --> 47:40.540
progress.

47:40.540 --> 47:41.580
That is so clear.

47:42.000 --> 47:46.600
And while the paper is primarily focused on that first axis, how we internally own our

47:46.600 --> 47:52.120
purposes, it explicitly acknowledges that the real world impact axis matters immensely.

47:52.360 --> 47:52.940
Yes, it does.

47:52.940 --> 47:57.980
To really drive this home and show how this plays out in normal life, the author provides

47:57.980 --> 47:59.440
four specific case studies.

47:59.780 --> 48:00.400
Let's walk through them.

48:00.620 --> 48:00.860
Okay.

48:01.360 --> 48:03.200
Case number one, the apprentice.

48:03.200 --> 48:09.700
This is the classic example of learning a physical, skilled trade, laying brick, hanging drywall,

48:09.920 --> 48:10.980
or framing a house.

48:11.200 --> 48:12.680
It's a lot of repetition.

48:12.980 --> 48:13.420
It is.

48:13.760 --> 48:17.480
At the very beginning, the repetition is pure, unadulterated tedium.

48:17.820 --> 48:19.060
It is a harsh grind.

48:19.720 --> 48:24.640
But gradually, through thousands of repetitions, the physical movements become automatic.

48:25.100 --> 48:26.160
So, encapsulation.

48:26.160 --> 48:28.600
Yes, this is successful encapsulation.

48:29.120 --> 48:35.020
And that encapsulation is what frees the apprentice's cognitive attention to notice the subtle, vital

48:35.020 --> 48:36.280
properties of the environment.

48:36.840 --> 48:39.980
How the specific grain of this wood will absorb moisture differently.

48:40.300 --> 48:43.160
Or how the foundation is sitting slightly out of plume.

48:43.840 --> 48:46.980
So, in this case, automaticity directly serves autonomy.

48:47.500 --> 48:51.420
The physical routine frees up the brain for higher-level, responsive judgment.

48:51.820 --> 48:52.200
Yes.

48:52.200 --> 48:58.720
But the failure mode, insulation, happens if the master of the shop dictates one rigid,

48:58.980 --> 49:03.820
unyielding method for every single condition, and treats any question or observation from

49:03.820 --> 49:05.580
the apprentice as insubordination.

49:06.340 --> 49:08.260
Oh, like, shut up and do what I tell you.

49:08.380 --> 49:08.740
Exactly.

49:09.100 --> 49:14.660
If the shop culture says, just lay the brick exactly this way, don't ask why, the apprentice's

49:14.660 --> 49:15.780
mind becomes insulated.

49:16.800 --> 49:22.140
The ultimate test of their autonomy is, what happens when the building code suddenly changes?

49:22.520 --> 49:24.600
Or a new material behaves differently?

49:24.800 --> 49:25.420
Do they adapt?

49:25.800 --> 49:25.980
Right.

49:26.760 --> 49:29.400
Does the apprentice know why they do what they do?

49:29.980 --> 49:32.960
Or do they only know how to execute the dead routine?

49:33.340 --> 49:34.320
That's a great test.

49:35.360 --> 49:37.400
Okay, case number two, the language learner.

49:37.520 --> 49:41.820
We touched on this slightly with the grammar drills, but the paper uses the incredibly relatable

49:41.820 --> 49:43.380
example of using flashcards.

49:43.380 --> 49:48.160
The vocabulary flashcard deck is a perfect miniature required ledger.

49:48.860 --> 49:53.000
You convert cleared cards into a feeling of academic standing or progress.

49:53.240 --> 49:54.740
It feels like you're accomplishing something.

49:55.060 --> 49:55.460
It does.

49:55.800 --> 50:00.740
But the great danger is that a learner might always review the deck in the exact same sequence.

50:01.600 --> 50:07.320
Over time, they end up recalling the target word not based on its actual linguistic meaning,

50:07.320 --> 50:10.600
but based simply on the card that came right before it.

50:10.700 --> 50:11.880
Oh, I've definitely done this.

50:12.660 --> 50:15.140
They memorize the sequence of the deck, not the language.

50:15.280 --> 50:17.580
Their brain optimized for the wrong variable.

50:17.920 --> 50:18.120
Right.

50:18.420 --> 50:21.280
They become insulated within the mechanics of the study tool.

50:22.240 --> 50:27.640
The paper notes that the simple, physical act of shuffling the deck forces the recall to

50:27.640 --> 50:28.760
depend on the word itself.

50:28.860 --> 50:29.680
It breaks the routine.

50:29.680 --> 50:31.640
It opens the inner base to reality.

50:32.260 --> 50:36.600
The learner who actively notices that a memorized flashcard definition doesn't quite match how

50:36.600 --> 50:41.260
native speakers actually use the word in a podcast, and then physically pulls out a pen

50:41.260 --> 50:46.360
and alters the card, that learner is exercising active supervision over their purpose.

50:46.660 --> 50:49.260
They are being responsive to evidence outside the ledger.

50:49.540 --> 50:50.020
Exactly.

50:50.440 --> 50:52.460
Which brings us to case three, the gamer.

50:52.840 --> 50:54.780
This is the ultimate modern grind.

50:54.780 --> 51:00.440
We established earlier that the loop, the meter, and the unlock all belong to the designer.

51:01.060 --> 51:03.620
The paper asks a critical modal question here.

51:04.200 --> 51:06.880
What happens when the designer fundamentally alters the game?

51:07.400 --> 51:12.540
What if they retire a powerful item or shut down a server, and the structural ground falls

51:12.540 --> 51:14.000
out from under the player's routine?

51:14.280 --> 51:16.580
Which happens all the time in online games.

51:16.760 --> 51:17.000
Yes.

51:17.420 --> 51:22.740
If the player just immediately migrates to seek the next externally supplied loop in a different

51:22.740 --> 51:26.120
game, it demonstrates that the mindless loop itself was the point.

51:26.600 --> 51:27.860
They are structurally dependent.

51:28.240 --> 51:30.380
But if they do something else...

51:30.380 --> 51:30.640
Right.

51:30.740 --> 51:35.500
If the player reacts by starting to build tools, writing mods, or creating their own community

51:35.500 --> 51:40.700
challenges outside the developer's rigid rules, they have taken ownership of the meter.

51:41.200 --> 51:44.520
They have transitioned from being an NPC to a protagonist.

51:44.520 --> 51:45.360
I love that.

51:46.060 --> 51:48.800
And finally, case four, the clerk.

51:48.800 --> 51:52.960
This is the figure Nietzsche himself called out, the slave of the dusk.

51:53.320 --> 51:58.620
Routine administrative work is philosophically tricky because a complex, well-run society

51:58.620 --> 52:01.380
actually desperately needs reliable procedures.

52:02.140 --> 52:06.960
You cannot have every single clerk at the Department of Motor Vehicles constantly agonizing over

52:06.960 --> 52:09.780
the existential purpose of every form they process.

52:09.980 --> 52:13.820
Oh man, the DMV would be even slower than it already is.

52:13.880 --> 52:15.820
A society would literally grind to a halt.

52:15.820 --> 52:16.340
Exactly.

52:16.840 --> 52:20.720
But the key distinction is whether the clerk remains ultimately answerable to the real

52:20.720 --> 52:23.400
world purpose the procedure is supposed to serve.

52:23.620 --> 52:23.760
Right.

52:24.120 --> 52:29.460
If a clerk notices that a specific mandatory form is consistently failing to capture the

52:29.460 --> 52:34.440
information it was designed to capture, or that it is causing unnecessary harm to citizens,

52:34.820 --> 52:39.760
and they actively report it and try to change it, they are supervising the procedure.

52:39.900 --> 52:40.840
They are being responsive.

52:40.840 --> 52:41.400
Yes.

52:41.840 --> 52:47.720
If they just keep blindly processing the defective form simply because it is the form, they are

52:47.720 --> 52:51.480
acting as a purely fungible, insulated occupant of a position.

52:52.000 --> 52:55.120
What's really nuanced here is that independence happens at different altitudes.

52:55.260 --> 52:55.840
It really does.

52:56.140 --> 53:00.640
Like, a clerk might be incredibly responsive and autonomous about the procedure, reporting

53:00.640 --> 53:05.020
the bad form to their boss, but completely blind and insulated regarding the institution,

53:05.260 --> 53:09.760
like never pausing to question if their specific department should even exist in the first place.

53:09.760 --> 53:11.060
That is a crucial takeaway.

53:11.520 --> 53:15.920
Procedural intelligence does not automatically equal broad existential independence.

53:16.120 --> 53:16.320
Right.

53:17.080 --> 53:21.960
You can be a protagonist at the procedural level, but a complete executor at the project level.

53:22.320 --> 53:27.920
You have to constantly ask yourself how far up the chain of command your supervisory capacity

53:27.920 --> 53:29.100
actually extends.

53:29.280 --> 53:34.840
So to our listener, I want to ask you directly, which of these four cases do you see in your

53:34.840 --> 53:36.480
own daily life right now?

53:37.100 --> 53:37.980
It's a good time to reflect.

53:37.980 --> 53:38.320
Yeah.

53:38.560 --> 53:42.500
Are you the apprentice building a spreadsheet without knowing why the client even needs

53:42.500 --> 53:42.740
it?

53:43.300 --> 53:48.500
Are you the language learner reading the flashcards in the exact same order every day, just trying

53:48.500 --> 53:49.560
to clear your inbox?

53:49.920 --> 53:51.620
Or are you shuffling the deck?

53:51.700 --> 53:54.360
Are you modifying the rules of your own workplace game?

53:54.920 --> 53:59.620
It is a profound and frankly intimidating self-audit to undertake.

54:00.400 --> 54:06.040
But before we conclude, the paper does something very rigorous and intellectually honest.

54:06.260 --> 54:06.620
What's that?

54:06.620 --> 54:12.320
It stress tests its own argument by anticipating and addressing four major objections.

54:12.760 --> 54:15.860
We really need to look at these limits to see if the theory holds water.

54:16.080 --> 54:16.320
Yes.

54:17.000 --> 54:17.660
The objections.

54:18.060 --> 54:18.460
Let's hit them.

54:18.940 --> 54:19.620
Objection one.

54:20.580 --> 54:24.360
Does this entire argument secretly condemn excellence?

54:24.720 --> 54:24.960
Right.

54:24.960 --> 54:31.880
Because true excellence in art, sports, or science requires thousands of hours of repetition

54:31.880 --> 54:32.620
and practice.

54:32.620 --> 54:38.880
If we are highly suspicious of the grind, are we essentially suspicious of mastery?

54:38.880 --> 54:41.520
The paper's reply is a definitive no.

54:41.920 --> 54:46.900
The argument absolutely does not target automaticity or repetition as enemies.

54:47.340 --> 54:50.320
It treats them as necessary enabling conditions.

54:50.440 --> 54:50.600
Right.

54:50.660 --> 54:51.400
Like we said earlier.

54:51.720 --> 54:52.000
Yes.

54:52.260 --> 54:55.140
The target of the critique is the subordination of judgment.

54:55.980 --> 55:00.520
The skilled trades and the fine arts are actually the perfect examples where deep repetition

55:00.520 --> 55:03.700
and high level judgment must develop together.

55:03.700 --> 55:04.100
Okay.

55:04.200 --> 55:04.840
That makes sense.

55:05.520 --> 55:06.100
Objection two.

55:06.740 --> 55:10.740
Does this whole theory rely on the outdated idea of a unified self?

55:11.060 --> 55:11.920
This one gets deep.

55:12.160 --> 55:12.340
Yeah.

55:12.440 --> 55:17.480
Because Nietzsche himself famously argued that the subject is a fiction and that human beings

55:17.480 --> 55:20.360
are actually just a chaotic multiplicity of competing drives.

55:20.880 --> 55:26.580
How can an agent supervise its purposes if there is no central unified agent in the control

55:26.580 --> 55:27.240
room of the brain?

55:27.240 --> 55:29.340
This is a deep philosophical objection.

55:29.440 --> 55:29.860
It is.

55:29.860 --> 55:35.800
The paper replies that the supervision of purpose does not require a magical little homunculus

55:35.800 --> 55:38.440
sitting in a control room in your brain making decisions.

55:38.760 --> 55:39.100
Thank goodness.

55:39.400 --> 55:39.600
Right.

55:40.400 --> 55:45.980
The standing disposition to keep a routine answerable to evidence is simply a feature of how an organism's

55:45.980 --> 55:47.140
various drives are organized.

55:48.060 --> 55:53.540
Nietzsche didn't believe the unified self was a given starting condition, but he did praise

55:53.540 --> 55:55.200
unity as an achievement.

55:55.200 --> 55:58.360
A hard-won organization of competing drives.

55:59.140 --> 55:59.920
So it's something you build.

56:00.080 --> 56:00.300
Yes.

56:00.960 --> 56:04.640
This criterion of responsiveness is just a measure of that achievement.

56:05.260 --> 56:05.440
Okay.

56:06.100 --> 56:06.780
Objection three.

56:07.360 --> 56:11.440
Aren't fungible roles absolutely necessary for a functioning large-scale society?

56:11.640 --> 56:13.040
We touched on this with the DMV.

56:13.100 --> 56:13.600
Exactly.

56:14.360 --> 56:18.840
If every sanitation worker or every nurse and every air traffic controller decided to creatively

56:18.840 --> 56:22.840
reinvent their job every single day, society would instantly collapse.

56:22.840 --> 56:26.560
Is this argument just utopian anti-work nonsense?

56:27.600 --> 56:31.960
The text completely agrees that society needs reliable role fillers.

56:32.280 --> 56:36.480
It is not advocating for anarchy where everyone abandons their posts.

56:36.640 --> 56:36.940
Good to know.

56:37.740 --> 56:42.520
What it asks is that the occupants of those vital roles retain the internal capacity to

56:42.520 --> 56:45.240
notice when the role stops serving its actual function.

56:45.240 --> 56:50.760
The structural interchangeability of occupants is completely compatible with the intellectual

56:50.760 --> 56:52.400
answerability of occupants.

56:52.600 --> 56:53.440
So you can do both.

56:53.620 --> 56:53.800
Yes.

56:54.120 --> 56:58.900
You can do the job exactly by the book, keeping society running, while simultaneously keeping

56:58.900 --> 57:02.240
your eyes wide open to whether the book itself needs rewriting.

57:02.460 --> 57:02.940
I love that.

57:03.420 --> 57:05.260
And objection four, the final one.

57:05.760 --> 57:10.760
Isn't the supposed responsiveness just a matter of luck or just putting on a good show?

57:10.760 --> 57:12.640
Ah, the display objection.

57:12.880 --> 57:12.980
Yeah.

57:13.040 --> 57:17.520
How do you separate true, deep supervision from someone who just got lucky with a random

57:17.520 --> 57:22.400
guess or someone who is just displaying fake performative independence to impress their

57:22.400 --> 57:22.740
boss?

57:23.080 --> 57:25.880
The paper falls back on its operational definition here.

57:26.720 --> 57:31.860
Display, acting like a pre-thinker, is ultimately insensitive to evidence.

57:32.580 --> 57:36.960
The person putting on a performative show will stubbornly keep doing it even when reality

57:36.960 --> 57:38.020
proves it fails.

57:38.300 --> 57:39.660
Because the performance is the point.

57:39.660 --> 57:40.180
Exactly.

57:40.780 --> 57:45.600
And luck, by definition, fails to recur across varied shifting circumstances.

57:46.220 --> 57:50.520
You might get lucky and look like a genius once, but you won't be consistently responsive

57:50.520 --> 57:52.940
to shifting realities over a decade.

57:53.380 --> 57:54.900
You can't fake it forever.

57:55.440 --> 57:55.700
Right.

57:55.960 --> 57:59.380
This is exactly why responsiveness must be judged retrospectively.

57:59.540 --> 58:04.160
You cannot just take a multiple choice personality test to prove you are responsive.

58:04.720 --> 58:09.100
You have to look at a long track record of how you handled actual difficult changes

58:09.100 --> 58:09.860
and circumstances.

58:10.260 --> 58:12.860
Which brings us to the ultimate limits of this framework.

58:13.240 --> 58:14.520
The paper notes two big ones.

58:14.720 --> 58:14.840
Yes.

58:15.040 --> 58:21.360
First, as we just said, it is just a retrospective criterion, not a magic diagnostic tool you can

58:21.360 --> 58:23.380
use in advance to predict the future.

58:23.880 --> 58:29.760
And second, and I think this is vital for modern readers, it aggressively separates Nietzsche's

58:29.760 --> 58:32.840
inherent historical elitism from the structural critique.

58:32.840 --> 58:34.600
Yes, that is so important.

58:35.100 --> 58:39.660
Nietzsche fundamentally believed that only a very few higher individuals were genetically

58:39.660 --> 58:42.040
or spiritually capable of this kind of independence.

58:42.840 --> 58:47.900
And that their existence justified the suffering of the massive unthinking herd.

58:48.120 --> 58:49.260
Which is pretty gross.

58:49.440 --> 58:49.840
It is.

58:50.060 --> 58:54.140
The paper explicitly says we do not need to accept that toxic hierarchy.

58:54.140 --> 59:00.100
To update the theory, it brings in Michel Foucault's concept of institutional discipline, the idea

59:00.100 --> 59:05.180
that modern institutions are structurally designed to produce docile and useful bodies.

59:05.740 --> 59:05.860
Right.

59:06.140 --> 59:10.240
Foucault's famous critique of how the architecture of the prison, the school, and the hospital

59:10.240 --> 59:11.800
are basically identical.

59:12.120 --> 59:12.520
Exactly.

59:13.020 --> 59:17.620
That kind of invisible structural discipline affects absolutely everyone in modern society,

59:17.920 --> 59:19.140
regardless of their supposed rank.

59:19.880 --> 59:21.100
We're all subject to it.

59:21.100 --> 59:21.500
Yes.

59:22.040 --> 59:27.220
Therefore, the paper argues, actively cultivating responsiveness to reasons is not just a philosophical

59:27.220 --> 59:29.080
luxury for a few elite thinkers.

59:29.600 --> 59:31.860
It is a baseline democratic necessity.

59:32.540 --> 59:32.880
Wow.

59:32.880 --> 59:37.860
We desperately need to examine our schools and our corporate workplaces to see if they are

59:37.860 --> 59:43.080
cultivating endurance only, or if they actually teach the structural grounds of the routine,

59:43.500 --> 59:48.620
expose those grounds to public inspection, and actively reward people who reconsider tasks

59:48.620 --> 59:50.360
when the point of the task is vanished.

59:50.360 --> 59:55.860
So this brings up the concept of the cost of exit, which I think is the single most relatable

59:55.860 --> 59:58.460
constraint for all of us living in the real world.

59:58.720 --> 59:58.940
Yes.

59:59.320 --> 01:00:00.780
The cost of exit is huge.

01:00:01.060 --> 01:00:06.220
In a video game, if the grind sucks, if the loop is unrewarding, and you realize you are

01:00:06.220 --> 01:00:10.480
just operating as an unpaid NPC for the designer, you can just log off.

01:00:10.560 --> 01:00:12.120
You just uninstall the software.

01:00:12.280 --> 01:00:12.780
It's that easy.

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:17.440
But in compulsory schooling, or in a corporate job that provides your family's health insurance

01:00:17.440 --> 01:00:20.140
and pays your mortgage, you cannot just log off.

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:22.240
The cost of exit is your livelihood.

01:00:22.700 --> 01:00:23.540
Maybe your survival.

01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:28.720
Which is exactly why cultivating this internal structural responsiveness is so crucial.

01:00:29.420 --> 01:00:33.580
When you are trapped in a system where you cannot easily change your external environment,

01:00:34.220 --> 01:00:38.960
your internal organization of purpose is the only armor protecting you from total cognitive

01:00:38.960 --> 01:00:39.640
insulation.

01:00:39.640 --> 01:00:41.120
That makes a lot of sense.

01:00:41.500 --> 01:00:46.060
If you are financially forced to perform the magnificent monotony, you must fight to keep

01:00:46.060 --> 01:00:46.980
your interfaces open.

01:00:47.120 --> 01:00:53.020
You must retain the private capacity to ask what the routine is actually for, even if you

01:00:53.020 --> 01:00:55.060
know you still have to wake up and perform it tomorrow.

01:00:55.580 --> 01:00:58.020
You can't let the system rewrite your code.

01:00:58.180 --> 01:00:58.560
Exactly.

01:00:59.220 --> 01:01:04.300
That internal SCARC is the difference between surviving a system and being completely erased

01:01:04.300 --> 01:01:04.740
by it.

01:01:04.740 --> 01:01:05.420
Wow.

01:01:05.780 --> 01:01:09.120
Okay, we have covered an incredible amount of conceptual ground today.

01:01:09.500 --> 01:01:11.000
Let's try to recap this journey.

01:01:11.320 --> 01:01:11.840
Let's do it.

01:01:12.240 --> 01:01:17.660
We started with the 19th century philologist swatting over Greek grammar, and we realized

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:22.460
that structure is identical to the modern gamer grinding for digital loot.

01:01:22.720 --> 01:01:22.980
Right.

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:29.540
We discovered that magnificent monotony, the sheer repetition of unrewarding tasks, isn't

01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:30.580
inherently evil.

01:01:31.000 --> 01:01:32.620
It can build societal endurance.

01:01:33.040 --> 01:01:38.460
It can provide necessary psychological relief for the exhausted, and it can build true mastery.

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:40.880
Automaticity is not the enemy.

01:01:40.880 --> 01:01:43.600
The enemy is unexamined obedience.

01:01:44.300 --> 01:01:48.920
The real danger is when our higher order judgment becomes totally subordinated to the automatic

01:01:48.920 --> 01:01:53.400
routine, when our minds become insulated from the reality of the world around us.

01:01:53.640 --> 01:01:59.740
We looked at the concept of the non-player character, not as a cheap internet insult to throw at people

01:01:59.740 --> 01:02:04.120
we disagree with, but as a precise diagnostic tool for our own lives.

01:02:04.120 --> 01:02:10.780
An NPC is someone whose purposes are fixed entirely from the outside and remain unavailable for

01:02:10.780 --> 01:02:11.200
revision.

01:02:11.980 --> 01:02:17.700
To break out of that trap, we have to achieve that third highest level of authorship, the

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:22.140
capacity to continuously revise our purposes based on relevant evidence.

01:02:23.240 --> 01:02:27.780
And that matters immensely for you, the listener, because it is a direct call to audit your own

01:02:27.780 --> 01:02:28.500
daily routines.

01:02:29.080 --> 01:02:32.420
Look at your ledger at work, your habits at home, your hobbies.

01:02:32.420 --> 01:02:34.900
Are your interfaces open?

01:02:35.380 --> 01:02:35.540
Yeah.

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:41.360
Are you actively shuffling your cognitive dex, or are you just reading the flashcards in the

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:43.260
exact same order every single day?

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:50.320
Are you operating as a fungible executor of a role, or are you keeping your routines answerable

01:02:50.320 --> 01:02:50.980
to reality?

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:54.040
It is a heavy set of questions to carry with you.

01:02:54.180 --> 01:02:54.840
It is.

01:02:55.360 --> 01:03:00.180
But it leaves me with one final thought, something to mull over that builds on everything Flyxion

01:03:00.180 --> 01:03:02.460
wrote here, but takes it one step further.

01:03:02.780 --> 01:03:03.660
Oh, okay.

01:03:04.120 --> 01:03:04.580
Let's hear it.

01:03:04.740 --> 01:03:05.500
Let's say we do it.

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:11.520
Let's say we successfully step out of the NPC role in our jobs, our hobbies, and we take

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:12.600
ownership of the rules.

01:03:12.600 --> 01:03:16.600
We keep our interfaces open, and we revise our purposes.

01:03:16.600 --> 01:03:22.300
Aren't we just entering a higher-level game designed by society?

01:03:23.000 --> 01:03:27.680
I mean, if you quit the soulless corporate grind to start your own independent business,

01:03:28.060 --> 01:03:34.240
you feel autonomous, but aren't you now just playing the larger, pre-designed game of market

01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:34.800
capitalism?

01:03:35.440 --> 01:03:36.400
Oh, that's interesting.

01:03:36.400 --> 01:03:42.480
Can we ever truly step completely outside the game, or is the ultimate human experience

01:03:42.480 --> 01:03:48.500
just the continuous, magnificent grind of discovering who designed the next level up?

01:03:49.140 --> 01:03:51.960
That is a phenomenal, unsettling question.

01:03:52.820 --> 01:03:58.520
It suggests that responsiveness might not be a final destination or a permanent state of

01:03:58.520 --> 01:04:04.400
enlightenment we arrive at, but rather a continuous, lifelong process of zooming out.

01:04:04.400 --> 01:04:09.240
Something to think deeply about while you are painting that fence, commuting to that

01:04:09.240 --> 01:04:11.740
office, or filling out that spreadsheet tomorrow.

01:04:12.460 --> 01:04:14.500
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

01:04:14.860 --> 01:04:18.280
Keep your interfaces open, shuffle your decks, and we will see you next time.

